=== REASONING ===

P2 ("tool-use as the only mutation path") and P4 ("strategy") are the
two foundational patterns this slice realises. The design preserves
ADR-015 by making every AI surface gated and auditable.

Key design decisions:

(a) Tool interface is owned in `internal/ai/tools` so any AI feature can
    register a tool without import-cycling against dispatch/strategy.
    Method shape (Name/Description/InputSchema/OutputSchema/Mutates/
    RequiredScope/Validate/Execute) matches the prompt's D5.1 verbatim.

(b) Schema generation reads `validate:"..."` struct tags via reflection
    (R2 mitigation). The same tag set drives runtime validation
    (`tools.ValidateStruct[T]`). Both halves walk a shared `parseValidateTag`
    rule map so a constraint added to one half MUST appear in the other —
    otherwise `TestEverySchemaMatchesHandlerValidation` breaks. The fuzz
    pin actually caught a real divergence during development:
    `query_drives_recent.limit` was tagged `gte=1,lte=100` (no `required`)
    but the validator rejected zero values. The fix was to make `limit`
    `required` explicitly so schema and validator agree. A real R2 catch.

(c) Strategy interface is shipped INTERFACE ONLY per the prompt. The
    `RedactionPolicy` and `EvalGoldens` return types are placeholder
    interfaces with non-exported markers (`policyMarker`, `goldenMarker`)
    so no code outside the strategy package can synthesise a "wrong"
    policy. F8 will widen `RedactionPolicy` with an `Apply(...)` method;
    F6 will widen `EvalGolden` with concrete fields. Both extensions are
    backwards-compatible because the placeholder is an interface.

(d) Dispatcher (D5.5) is split across three files for readability:
    `dispatch.go` (the Run loop), `continuation.go` (paused-state shape),
    `stream.go` (StreamWriter interface + a CaptureWriter for tests).
    The chat loop is the obvious one: provider.Chat → optional tool
    calls → validate → optional confirm → execute → repeat → stop OR
    MaxIterations. Streaming (Provider.Stream) lands with F5; F4 calls
    Chat() and forwards the response content as a single delta so the
    contract is testable.

(e) Confirm flow (D5.5 second half) uses a `ConfirmFn` callback. In
    production the wiring will wrap an SSE round-trip:
    dispatcher emits a confirm_request frame → frontend renders
    AiConfirmDialog → user clicks → frontend POSTs the decision →
    callback returns. In tests the callback is a synchronous fake.
    The continuation TABLE (`ai_chat_continuations`) persists state for
    runs that span the SSE boundary so a network blip between
    "user clicked Confirm" and "dispatcher resumed" is recoverable.

(f) Migration slot 000204 (NOT 000199 as the prompt header suggests).
    Slot 000199 is occupied by `si_aware_convert_functions` from
    Phase-48; F0..F3 occupy 000201..000203. The slot-variance precedent
    is set by F0..F3's logs.

(g) 12 starter tools wrap REPOS not handlers. Handlers are HTTP-bound
    (parse URL params, write JSON, manage status codes); repos are
    where the actual data access lives. The R2 mitigation still holds:
    each tool defines a NEW typed Input DTO in the tools package with
    `validate:"..."` tags; both the schema generator and the runtime
    validator reflect from those tags. Nothing about the tool→repo
    boundary opens a new SQL surface — every method called is a
    pre-existing typed repo method.

(h) Mutating tools are NOT shipped in this slice. Per the prompt:
    "Mutating tools are NOT shipped in this slice — they belong with
    the features that use them (N1, N2, etc.)." All 12 builtins return
    `Mutates() == false`. The dispatcher's confirm-flow code paths are
    therefore exercised ONLY by tests (using `pingTool{mutates:true}`).
    `TestBuiltinsHaveNoMutators` pins this contract.

(i) Continuations table cleanup: `CleanupExpired` is a repo method, NOT
    a worker tick. The methodology document allows a "wired by the next
    worker-touching slice" deferral. The architecture doc and the
    inline `// 24h cleanup` comment make the deferral explicit.

(j) The ESLint AI-import rule from F0 only blocks `internal/ai/provider`
    imports outside designated AI surfaces. New `internal/ai/{tools,
    strategy,dispatch}` packages are already inside `internal/ai/...`
    so the rule allows them. No ESLint changes needed.

