=== AI-OFF CONTRACT ===

ADR-015 invariant evidence (only invariants this slice touches):

I1 (default-off):
  PASS — F4 adds NEW packages (internal/ai/{tools,strategy,dispatch})
         and a NEW table (ai_chat_continuations) but registers ZERO
         feature toggles, ZERO HTTP routes, and zero SSE endpoints.
         The feature registry is unchanged: aivet reports
         "3 feature(s) in registry" (chatbot-llm, ai-provider-health,
         __usage__) — same count as before. So no new AI surface
         is reachable in any deployment, regardless of ai_mode.
  EVIDENCE: aigen --check reports "in sync" (no regenerate needed);
            aivet reports "5 AI route(s), 3 feature(s)" (counts
            preserved from F3). git diff internal/ai/features/
            registry.go is empty. git diff internal/api/router.go
            is empty.

I3 (baseline intact):
  PASS — Zero non-AI files modified by this slice. The new
         internal/ai/* packages are unreachable from non-AI code
         paths. The new ai_chat_continuations table is empty until
         a mutating tool actually pauses (no mutating tools shipped
         here). The new docs/architecture/ai-tool-use.md is doc-only.
  EVIDENCE: git diff in `=== COMMIT ===` lists 10 paths, all
            within internal/ai/, internal/database/ai_chat_*,
            migrations/000204_*, web/src/components/ai/, and
            docs/architecture/. No baseline page, repo, handler,
            or model is touched.

I4 (zero outbound egress):
  PASS — F4 ships infrastructure that COULD be used to make outbound
         calls (the Dispatcher invokes provider.Provider.Chat) but
         the dispatcher is not wired into any HTTP route this slice
         registers. Every test uses a scriptedProvider or Mock —
         no network code path is reachable.
         Furthermore: the 12 starter tools are READ-ONLY and call
         only LOCAL repos (PostgreSQL via the existing pgx pool).
         No tool initiates an outbound HTTP request.
  EVIDENCE: grep -r "http.Get\|http.Post\|net/http" internal/ai/tools/
            internal/ai/dispatch/ internal/ai/strategy/ returns
            no production-code matches. The CaptureWriter and
            scripted provider in tests are pure in-memory.

I5 (hidden UI):
  PASS — AiConfirmDialog is shipped but NOT mounted by any page in
         this slice. It will be consumed by per-feature pages (N1,
         N2, ...) which themselves are gated by withAiFeature. Until
         a parent component renders it with open=true, the dialog
         is dead weight. The Modal it wraps respects open=false by
         returning null.
  EVIDENCE: grep -r "AiConfirmDialog" web/src/ outside the
            component file + its test returns no matches. No new
            <withAiFeature> wrappers exist; no new pages exist.

I6 (404 routes preserved):
  PASS — Zero new HTTP routes added. aivet reports the same 5 AI
         route count as the baseline. The dispatcher's eventual SSE
         endpoint is F5's responsibility; F4 ships only the
         in-process plumbing.
  EVIDENCE: aivet output: "OK — 5 AI route(s), 3 feature(s) in
            registry, TS mirror in sync".

I8 (audit survives downgrade):
  PASS — The new ai_chat_continuations table holds dispatcher state
         for paused mutating-tool calls. Rows have a 24h hard
         expiry but they are NOT cascade-deleted on subject removal;
         user_subject is a TEXT column with no FK (matching the
         ai_call_log convention from F3). An operator running the
         CleanupExpired tick sees stale rows GC'd by time, not by
         user removal — so a forensic "what conversation did this
         user almost run?" query against expires_at>now() always
         returns whatever's still in flight.
  EVIDENCE: migrations/000204 has no FOREIGN KEY clause anywhere;
            grep "REFERENCES" migrations/000204_*.sql returns no
            matches.

I9 (provenance visible):
  PASS — AiConfirmDialog renders the tool name + JSON arguments
         emitted by the LLM verbatim in a <pre> block. The user
         sees exactly what is about to happen before approving.
         Test "renders tool name and pretty-printed args when open"
         asserts the JSON renders with field names + values intact.
  EVIDENCE: vitest case "renders tool name and pretty-printed
            args when open" expects("rule_id": 42) AND
            ("threshold": 80). The component code passes
            `JSON.stringify(args ?? {}, null, 2)` to the <pre>
            so no transformation occurs.

I10 (per-feature isolation in dispatcher):
  PASS — Strategy.Tools() is a hard whitelist enforced by the
         dispatcher BEFORE the LLM sees the tool catalogue. Even
         if an LLM hallucinates a tool the strategy didn't declare,
         the dispatcher returns
         "tool ... not allowed for this strategy" rather than
         executing. Test
         TestDispatcher_ToolNotInStrategyAllowlistRejected pins
         this contract.
  EVIDENCE: dispatch_test.go::TestDispatcher_ToolNotInStrategyAllowlistRejected
            scripts an LLM that proposes "echo" while the strategy
            only allows "ping"; the test asserts the result is a
            tool_error event and execution does not occur.

R1 (write-path corruption):
  N/A — F4 writes nothing to existing typed tables. The only NEW
        table is ai_chat_continuations whose payload is opaque
        JSONB containing dispatcher state; it is read back only by
        the dispatcher itself.

R2 (schema drift between schema and validator):
  PASS — TestEverySchemaMatchesHandlerValidation runs over every
         registered builtin tool and asserts the JSON-Schema
         declared "required" + additionalProperties=false constraints
         AGREE with the runtime validator's accept/reject decisions.
         The fuzz test caught a real divergence during development
         (limit=0 accepted by schema, rejected by validator) and the
         fix (adding `required` to the limit tag in three tools)
         was applied before the gate was claimed green.
  EVIDENCE: fuzz_schema_test.go::TestEverySchemaMatchesHandlerValidation
            runs 12 sub-tests, all passing.

R5 (P2 — LLM cannot write raw SQL):
  PASS — The dispatcher refuses to call db.Exec on the LLM's behalf.
         The LLM's only path to mutation is through tool.Execute;
         tool.Validate runs first; mutating tools require user
         confirm via ConfirmFn. Test
         TestDispatcher_ConfirmDeniedAbortsRun pins that a denied
         confirm aborts the run with ErrConfirmationDenied — no
         tool.Execute is reached.
  EVIDENCE: dispatch.go runOneToolCall implementation; grep
            "db.Exec" or "Pool.Exec" inside internal/ai/tools/,
            internal/ai/dispatch/, internal/ai/strategy/ returns
            no matches.

